## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to architect provenance-aware prompt pipelines at scale, ensuring data lineage, watermarking, and auditability across tenants with realistic latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Data provenance and lineage in LLM prompts
- Per-tenant guards, watermarking, policy enforcement
- Output verification with source citations via a mock knowledge graph
- Structured audit logging: input hash, rewritten prompt, per-model outputs, latency, drift verdict
- Security considerations around prompt leakage and PII

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
function rewriteWithProvenance(prompt, tenantPolicy) {
  const provenance = `Source=${tenantPolicy.allowedSources.join('|')};Tenant=${tenantPolicy.id}`;
  return `${prompt}\n\n[Provenance:${provenance}]`;
}
function verifyOutput(output, sources) {
  const hasCitation = sources.some(s => output.includes(s));
  return hasCitation;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle non-determinism across models while preserving provenance guarantees?
- How would you extend the audit log to support compliance reviews and exception handling?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a scalable, secure prompt pipeline that enforces per-tenant policies, blocks prompt-injection, and records privacy-preserving audits in real time.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy schemas (allowed_actions, data_classifications)
- Safety shims against system-prompt leakage and prompt-injection
- Privacy-preserving reasoning traces in audit logs
- Latency budgeting and streaming guarantees

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype components for the three stages
function validatePolicy(prompt, tenantPolicy) {
  // check allowed_actions and data_classifications
  const okAction = tenantPolicy.allowed_actions.includes('execute') || prompt.includes('export');
  const safeData = !/SECRET|PASSWORD/i.test(prompt);
  return okAction && safeData;
}

function guardPrompt(prompt, policy) {
  // simple sanitizer to remove system prompts or injected cues
  let p = prompt.replace(/system prompt:.*$/i, '').trim();
  return p;
}

function logAudit(input, modelVersion, latency, reasoning) {
  return {
    input: input, modelVersion, latency, reasoningTrace: reasoning
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this against non-determinism and ensure consistent audits at scale?
- What metrics would you collect to detect policy drift or leakage risk over time?

